WGET.EXE: downloads over HTTP and HTTPS for DOS, on vmdos's own TCP.

    WGET [-k] [-q] [--ca FILE] URL [FILE]

wget.c is the program (vmdos's own; the same file builds on Linux to test
it there). Under DOS its sockets are ssh's (third_party/openssh/dos-sock.c:
socket(), connect(), getaddrinfo() on vmdos's TCP through INT 2Fh AX=564Eh,
VMTCP.CFG's addresses and DNS servers, IPv4 and IPv6); dos/ has the little
dos-sock.c needs from the rest of the ssh port. TLS 1.2 and 1.3 are
mbedTLS's (github.com/Mbed-TLS/mbedtls 3.6, Apache-2.0), its default
configuration less what DOS can't use (wget_mbedtls_config.h), entropy from
RDRAND, the TSC's jitter and the clock. Certificates are checked against
C:\VMDOS\NET\CACERT.PEM (third_party/cacert) and the host name; -k skips
that. HTTP/1.1, chunked bodies, up to 8 redirects. Nothing of it is in
vmdos's kernel.

Build: tools/build-wget.sh writes bin/WGET.EXE; the Makefile puts it in
C:\VMDOS\NET when it's there. DJGPP is a cross gcc on PATH
(i586-pc-msdosdjgpp-gcc), or else Docker's djfdyuruiry/djgpp image
(DJGPP_IMAGE= for another; DJGPP_DOCKER=1 to use Docker even with a
cross gcc). It also needs git, a host gcc with -m32 and upx.
On Linux, to test: build mbedTLS's library/*.c with
-DMBEDTLS_USER_CONFIG_FILE='"wget_mbedtls_config.h"' -I third_party/wget,
then  gcc wget.c libmbedtls.a  with the same flags.
